To reduce the total length of rope used, rope $HC$ and rope $HD$ are to be replaced by a single rope $HP$ where $P$ is a point on the straight line between $C$ and $D$. (The balloon remains at the same position $H$ above $O$ as described above.)  Determine the greatest length of rope that can be saved.

solution:
To save the most rope, we must have $HP$ having minimum length.
For $HP$ to have minimum length, $HP$ must be perpendicular to $CD$. [asy]
pair C, D, H, P;
H=(90,120);
C=(0,0);
D=(140,0);
P=(90,0);
draw(H--C--D--H--P);
label("H", H, N);
label("C", C, SW);
label("D", D, SE);
label("P", P, S);
label("150", (C+H)/2, NW);
label("130", (D+H)/2, NE);
[/asy] (Among other things, we can see from this diagram that sliding $P$ away from the perpendicular position does make $HP$ longer.)
In the diagram, $HC=150$, $HD=130$ and $CD=140$.
Let $HP=x$ and $PD=a$.  Then $CP=140-a$.
By the Pythagorean Theorem in $\triangle HPC$, $x^2 + (140-a)^2 = 150^2$.
By the Pythagorean Theorem in $\triangle HPD$, $x^2+a^2 = 130^2$.
Subtracting the second equation from the first, we obtain \begin{align*}
(140-a)^2 - a^2 & = 150^2 - 130^2 \\
(19600 - 280a+a^2)-a^2 & = 5600 \\
19600 -280a & = 5600 \\
280a & = 14000 \\
a & = 50
\end{align*} Therefore, $x^2 + 90^2 = 150^2$ or $x^2 = 150^2 - 90^2 = 22500 - 8100 = 14400$ so $x =120$.
So the shortest possible rope that we can use is 120 m, which saves $130+150-120 = \boxed{160}$ m of rope.

problem:
Equilateral triangle $DEF$ is inscribed in equilateral triangle $ABC$ as shown with $\overline{ED}\perp\overline{BC}.$ What is the ratio of the area of $\triangle DEF$ to the area of $\triangle
ABC$? [asy]
import olympiad; import geometry; size(100); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));
draw(dir(90)--dir(210)--(dir(-30))--cycle);
pair[] inters = intersectionpoints(Circle(origin,1/sqrt(3)),dir(90)--dir(210)--(dir(-30))--cycle);
inters[5] = dir(210) + ((dir(-30) - dir(210))/3);
draw(inters[0]--inters[2]--inters[5]--cycle);
draw(rightanglemark(inters[2],inters[5],dir(210),4));
dot("$A$",dir(90),N);
dot("$C$",dir(210),W);
dot("$B$",dir(-30),E);
dot("$F$",inters[0],E);
dot("$E$",inters[2],W);
dot("$D$",inters[5],S);
[/asy]

solution:
Since $\triangle CDE$ has a right angle at $D$ and $\angle C = 60^\circ,$ we can let $CD = x,$ $DE = x\sqrt{3},$ and $CE = 2x$ for some positive $x.$
[asy]
import olympiad; import geometry; size(100); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));
draw(dir(90)--dir(210)--(dir(-30))--cycle);
pair[] inters = intersectionpoints(Circle(origin,1/sqrt(3)),dir(90)--dir(210)--(dir(-30))--cycle);
inters[5] = dir(210) + ((dir(-30) - dir(210))/3);
draw(inters[0]--inters[2]--inters[5]--cycle);
draw(rightanglemark(inters[2],inters[5],dir(210),4));
dot("$A$",dir(90),N);
dot("$C$",dir(210),W);
dot("$B$",dir(-30),E);
dot("$F$",inters[0],E);
dot("$E$",inters[2],W);
dot("$D$",inters[5],S);
label("$x$",dir(210)--inters[5],S);
label("$2x$",dir(210)--inters[2],W);
label("$x$",inters[2]--dir(90),W);
[/asy]Note that $\triangle AEF \cong \triangle CDE,$ because $\angle AEF = 180^\circ - \angle DEF - \angle CED = 180^\circ - 60^\circ - 30^\circ = 90^\circ,$ $\angle EAF = 60^\circ,$ and $EF = DE.$ Then $AE = CD = x,$ so the side length of $\triangle ABC$ is $AC = AE + EC = 2x + x = 3x.$

Finally, the ratio of the areas of the triangles is the square of the ratio of the side lengths: $$\left(\frac{DE}{AC}\right)^2=\left(\frac{x\sqrt{3}}{3x}\right)^2=\boxed{\frac 13}.$$

