problem:
The grid below contains the $16$ points whose $x$- and $y$-coordinates are in the set $\{0,1,2,3\}$: [asy]
size(2.5cm);
for(int i=0; i<4; i+=1) { for(int j=0; j<4; j+=1) { dot((i,j)); }; };
[/asy] A square with all four of its vertices among these $16$ points has area $A$. What is the sum of all possible values of $A$?

solution:
To form a square with its vertices on the grid, we can start with a $1\times 1$, $2\times 2$, or $3\times 3$ square, then (optionally) cut off four congruent right triangles whose legs add up to the side length of the square we started with. These are all possible ways we can do it (up to congruence): [asy]
size(7cm);
path a=(1,1)--(2,1)--(2,2)--(1,2)--cycle;
path b=(5,1)--(6,0)--(7,1)--(6,2)--cycle;
path c=(10,0)--(12,0)--(12,2)--(10,2)--cycle;
path d=(15,1)--(17,0)--(18,2)--(16,3)--cycle;
path e=(20,0)--(23,0)--(23,3)--(20,3)--cycle;
fill(a, gray); draw(a);
draw((5,0)--(7,0)--(7,2)--(5,2)--(5,0),dashed);
fill(b, gray); draw(b);
fill(c, gray); draw(c);
draw((15,0)--(18,0)--(18,3)--(15,3)--(15,0),dashed);
fill(d, gray); draw(d);
fill(e, gray); draw(e);
for(int i=0; i<4; i+=1) { for(int j=0; j<4; j+=1) { dot((i,j)); dot((i+5,j)); dot((i+10,j)); dot((i+15,j)); dot((i+20,j)); }; };
[/asy] The areas are $1$, $2$, $4$, $5$, and $9$. (In the case of the second and fourth squares, we can compute these areas by subtracting the areas of the right triangles from the area of the squares indicated by the dashed lines. Or, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the side length of each square, then square this to get the area.)

The sum of all possible areas is $1+2+4+5+9=\boxed{21}$.

Let $O$ be the center of the sphere, and assume for now that $O$ is inside polyhedron $P$. We can carve polyhedron $P$ into pyramids, each of which has a face of $P$ as its base and $O$ as its apex. For example, a cube would be carved into six pyramids, two of which are highlighted in this drawing: [asy]
size(4cm);
import three;
triple A,B,C,D,EE,F,G,H;
A = (0,0,0);
B = (1,0,0);
C = (1,1,0);
D= (0,1,0);
EE = (0,0,1);
F = B+EE;
G = C + EE;
H = D + EE;
O = G/2;
draw(surface(B--O--C--cycle),red,nolight);
draw(surface(C--O--D--cycle),red+white,nolight);
draw(surface(H--O--G--cycle),lightblue,nolight);
draw(surface(G--O--F--cycle),blue,nolight);
draw(surface(EE--F--G--H--cycle),lightblue+blue,nolight);
draw(B--C--D);
draw(B--A--D,dashed);
draw(EE--F--G--H--EE);
draw(A--EE,dashed);
draw(B--F);
draw(C--G);
draw(D--H);
draw(A--O--C,dashed);
draw(B--O--D,dashed);
draw(EE--O--G,dashed);
draw(F--O--H,dashed);
dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(EE); dot(F); dot(G); dot(H); dot(O);
label("$O$",O,WSW);
[/asy] Then if we add up the areas of all the pyramids' bases, we get the surface area of $P$. If we add up the volumes of the pyramids, we get the volume of $P$.

The volume of each pyramid is equal to $\frac 13\cdot\text{(area of base)}\cdot\text{(height)}$. The height of each pyramid must be less than $36$, since the height of each pyramid extends from $O$ to a point inside the sphere. Therefore, the volume of each pyramid is less than $12$ times the area of the base. It follows that the volume of $P$ is less than $12$ times the surface area of $P$. We can, however, make this ratio arbitrarily close to $12$ by selecting polyhedra $P$ with many small faces, so that the height of each pyramid is as close as we wish to $36$.

Therefore, for polyhedra inscribed in a sphere of radius $36$ such that the center of the sphere lies inside the polyhedron, the least upper bound on $$\frac{\text{volume of }P}{\text{surface area of }P}$$is $12$. Finally, we must consider the case of inscribed polyhedra for which the center of the sphere does not lie inside the polyhedron. However, in this case, we can still construct pyramids with apex $O$ whose bases are the faces of $P$; then the surface area of $P$ is still the sum of the areas of the bases, but the volume of $P$ is less than the total volume of the pyramids. This only strengthens the argument for an upper bound of $12$. So, the answer is $\boxed{12}$.

We can write the equation as $z^{12} = 2^6,$ so the solutions are of the form
\[z = \sqrt{2} \operatorname{cis} \frac{2 \pi k}{12},\]where $0 \le k \le 11.$  These solutions are equally spaced on the circle with radius $\sqrt{2}.$

[asy]
unitsize(1.5 cm);

int i;

draw(Circle((0,0),sqrt(2)));
draw((-2,0)--(2,0));
draw((0,-2)--(0,2));

for (i = 0; i <= 11; ++i) {
  dot(sqrt(2)*dir(30*i));
}

label("$\sqrt{2}$", (sqrt(2)/2,0), S);
[/asy]

Noting that the imaginary parts cancel due to symmetry, the sum of the solutions with positive real part is then
\begin{align*}
&\sqrt{2} \operatorname{cis} \left( -\frac{\pi}{3} \right) + \sqrt{2} \operatorname{cis} \left( -\frac{\pi}{6} \right) + \sqrt{2} \operatorname{cis} 0 + \sqrt{2} \operatorname{cis} \frac{\pi}{6} + \sqrt{2} \operatorname{cis} \frac{\pi}{3} \\
&= \sqrt{2} \cos \frac{\pi}{3} + \sqrt{2} \cos \frac{\pi}{6} + \sqrt{2} \cos 0 + \sqrt{2} \cos \frac{\pi}{6} + \sqrt{2} \cos \frac{\pi}{3} \\
&= \boxed{2 \sqrt{2} + \sqrt{6}}.
\end{align*}

